1: Does this segment produce new knowledge, or does it consume and repackage knowledge created elsewhere?
Paragraph 1 excerpt: Soon after the necklace splitting problem was originally introduced [ 6], Goldberg and West [ 9] 88 proved that a solution always exists if k= 2.
Paragraph 1 excerpt: Alon and West [ 3] gave a simpler proof using 89 the Borsuk–Ulam theorem [ 7], and Alon [ 1] generalized the results to k>2.
Paragraph 1 excerpt: Finally, Alon 90 and Graur [ 2] discovered an efficient approximation algorithm for finding a solution with few 91 cuts.
Paragraph 1 excerpt: Alon and Graur [ 2] also consider an online variation of the problem.
Paragraph 1 excerpt: They derive lower 92 and upper bounds on the number of cuts needed when k= 2based on the value of n.
Paragraph 1 excerpt: They also generalize some of these results to the k>2case.

