"Soon after the necklace splitting problem was originally introduced [ 6], Goldberg and West [ 9] 88 proved that a solution always exists if k= 2. Alon and West [ 3] gave a simpler proof using 89 the Borsuk–Ulam theorem [ 7], and Alon [ 1] generalized the results to k>2. Finally, Alon 90 and Graur [ 2] discovered an efficient approximation algorithm for finding a solution with few 91 cuts. Alon and Graur [ 2] also consider an online variation of the problem. They derive lower 92 and upper bounds on the number of cuts needed when k= 2based on the value of n. They 93 also generalize some of these results to the k>2case. 94 In addition to necklace splitting, other fair division problems can be extended to dynamic 95 settings. Kash et al. [ 13] study a fair division problem with divisible goods where agents with 96 Leontiefpreferencesarriveovertimeandgoodsmustbeirrevocablyallocated. Benadeetal.[ 5] 97 study the problem of allocating indivisible goods that arrive in an online manner, again with 98 irrevocable decisions. He et al. [ 11] generalize this to a setting where reallocation is allowed 99 but expensive."

